window.MODULE_INTRODUCTIONS={
 sensitivities:{title:'Risk sensitivities',paragraphs:[
 'A financial instrument changes value when the market changes. A bond can fall in price when interest rates rise, a credit hedge can gain when a borrower’s credit spread widens, and an option can become more valuable when uncertainty increases. Risk sensitivities describe how much of that change comes from a particular market input.',
 'This module introduces DV01 and CS01 for interest-rate and credit-spread changes, followed by delta, gamma and vega for options. It then explains curvature, which measures how a larger move departs from a straight-line approximation. You will compare bonds, swaps, credit default swaps, equities and options rather than assume that the same sensitivity has the same sign for every instrument.',
 'Begin by changing one market input while keeping the others fixed. Compare the repriced value with the sensitivity approximation, and notice when the approximation becomes less reliable. By the end, you should be able to explain what a hedge protects, what it leaves exposed, and why the size and units of a reported sensitivity matter. The examples use simplified pricing models, so their assumptions remain visible beside each calculation.'
 ],start:'dv01',related:['yield','stochastic','frtb']},
 stochastic:{title:'Stochastic calculus',paragraphs:[
 'Stochastic calculus is a way to describe quantities that evolve over time with an uncertain component. In finance, these quantities might be an asset price, an interest rate or the value of a trading strategy. The aim is not to predict the next random movement exactly. It is to describe possible movements consistently and understand their consequences for valuation and risk.',
 'The first lessons use coin flips to introduce probability, expectation and the information available at a particular time. The path then moves from discrete random walks to Brownian motion, which provides a continuous-time model of uncertainty. You will see why ordinary calculus needs an additional term when it is applied to these paths, and how the Itô integral and Itô formula account for it.',
 'The later lessons connect this mathematics to replication, risk-neutral pricing, Black–Scholes valuation, changes of probability measure and pricing equations. Mean reversion, stopping times and jumps show where a simple continuous stock-price model is no longer enough. Follow the path in order if these ideas are new to you. Each chart is an illustration of the stated model; one simulated path is not evidence that a market will follow that path.'
 ],start:'probability',related:['sensitivities','yield']},
 frtb:{title:'FRTB market risk',paragraphs:[
 'The Fundamental Review of the Trading Book, usually called FRTB, is a framework for measuring the capital a bank needs against market risk in its trading book. Market prices can move, issuers can default, and some payoffs carry risks that ordinary sensitivities do not describe well. The framework therefore uses several components rather than treating every possible loss as the same problem.',
 'The standardised approach begins with sensitivities-based capital for delta, vega and curvature risk. It also includes a default risk charge and a residual risk add-on. You will explore the risk classes, see how positions are assigned to buckets, and follow the effect of weights, correlations and permitted offsets. The trade explorer connects familiar instruments to the components that may capture their risks.',
 'The internal models approach uses a different calculation route and requires supervisory permission. Its lessons cover expected shortfall, liquidity horizons, stress calibration, modellability, data quality, profit-and-loss attribution and backtesting. These tests matter because a plausible model result alone does not establish that a desk is eligible to use the approach.',
 'Start with the capital map, then work through the standardised calculations before moving to the internal models lessons. The module uses the identified PRA instrument as its principal reference and explains the scope of each example. A model input, an internal assumption and a prescribed regulatory parameter are distinguished throughout. The separate regulation module lets you compare the selected PRA, US proposal and Basel documents.'
 ],start:'frtb-map',related:['sensitivities','regulation','simm']},
 cva:{title:'SA-CVA counterparty risk',paragraphs:[
 'A derivative is a promise to exchange future payments. If the counterparty fails when it owes the bank money, the bank may not recover the full amount. Credit valuation adjustment, or CVA, reduces a default-free valuation to reflect this expected loss. CVA can change before any default occurs because the counterparty’s credit spread or the market factors driving exposure have changed.',
 'This module first explains CVA itself, including future positive exposure, credit curves, netting, collateral and wrong-way risk. It then introduces the standardised approach to CVA risk capital, or SA-CVA. That capital calculation measures sensitivity to changes in CVA and eligible hedges; it is a different quantity from the current CVA valuation adjustment or the capital held against counterparty default exposure.',
 'The six risk-class laboratories show how sensitivities and eligible hedges are weighted and aggregated. As you use them, check whether you are changing a valuation input, a hedge or a regulatory calculation input. Finish with the total-capital and governance lessons to understand how the pieces fit together. The examples follow the cited PRA provisions and state the factors and assumptions omitted from each reduced portfolio.'
 ],start:'cva-value',related:['saccr','xva','sensitivities']},
 saccr:{title:'SA-CCR counterparty exposure',paragraphs:[
 'A derivative can have little value today and still create a substantial exposure tomorrow. The standardised approach for counterparty credit risk, or SA-CCR, estimates a regulatory exposure amount by combining current replacement cost with an allowance for possible future exposure. The resulting exposure at default is an input to a capital calculation; it is not itself the capital charge.',
 'The path begins with netting and collateral because the legal agreement determines which positions can be considered together. It then develops replacement cost, the potential future exposure multiplier, adjusted notionals, supervisory delta and maturity factors. The asset-class examples show how those trade-level inputs feed the relevant hedging sets and aggregate add-ons.',
 'Pay particular attention to the difference between a market sensitivity and a supervisory parameter. SA-CCR deliberately uses prescribed conventions, so a supervisory delta is not a substitute for the hedge ratio from a trading model. Use the portfolio and governance lessons to connect individual examples to the overall exposure calculation, including the stated PRA alpha treatment and the boundaries of the simplified cases.'
 ],start:'saccr-map',related:['cva','xva','simm']},
 xva:{title:'XVA valuation adjustments',paragraphs:[
 'A textbook derivative price often assumes that payments are certain, funding is available on simple terms and trading does not consume capital or collateral resources. Real transactions involve counterparties that can default, collateral agreements, funding costs and other constraints. XVA is a collective name for valuation adjustments used to represent these effects under a specified valuation framework.',
 'The module separates counterparty credit, own credit, funding, collateral, initial margin and capital so that you can see the economic question behind each adjustment. Later lessons examine how these effects interact with netting, wrong-way risk, clearing, reserves and tax. Several adjustments can depend on the same cash flows, which means they cannot always be calculated independently and added without checking for overlap.',
 'Begin with the introductory valuation example and use the sign conventions shown in each lesson. Ask who is paying, who is receiving and which future cash flow is being valued. The applets illustrate mechanisms under explicit assumptions; they do not claim that every institution should use one universal XVA formula. The cited research explains the frameworks behind the more advanced examples.'
 ],related:['cva','saccr','simm','treasury-risk']},
 irrbb:{title:'Interest-rate risk in the banking book',paragraphs:[
 'A bank often makes loans whose rates are fixed for several years while funding them with deposits that can reprice or leave much sooner. Interest-rate risk in the banking book, or IRRBB, arises when changes in rates affect the bank’s earnings or the economic value of these assets and liabilities. It includes differences in repricing dates, differences between reference rates and options exercised by customers.',
 'This module uses two complementary views. Net interest income measures the effect on interest earnings over a defined period. Economic value of equity compares the present value of asset and liability cash flows over their remaining lives. A decision can improve one view while worsening the other, so neither should be interpreted in isolation.',
 'You will build cash flows, apply rate shocks and explore deposit behaviour, prepayments, basis risk and hedges. Later examples connect the calculations to supervisory shock conventions, reporting and governance. Start with the overview, then change one balance-sheet feature at a time. The module identifies whether an input is a behavioural assumption, a simplified model choice or part of the cited PRA and Basel framework.'
 ],related:['yield','treasury-risk','liquidity']},
 yield:{title:'Yield curves and bootstrapping',paragraphs:[
 'A payment due next month and a payment due in ten years should not normally be discounted using the same single rate. A yield curve describes the relationship between time and the rates or discount factors used to value future payments. Building a curve connects observable market quotes to the prices and risks of loans, bonds, swaps and other instruments.',
 'Bootstrapping works through suitable market instruments in sequence. Once earlier discount factors are known, the next instrument can help determine an additional point on the curve. The module also distinguishes spot rates from forward rates and shows how interpolation fills the gaps between quoted maturities. Different interpolation choices can change valuations and sensitivities even when the original quotes are matched.',
 'Start with discount factors and then follow the quote-to-curve calculations. Change a quote, rebuild the curve and examine the resulting valuation change. The later examples explain overnight-index discounting and the use of separate curves for projecting and discounting cash flows. The simplified instruments and compounding conventions are stated explicitly so that the arithmetic can be followed.'
 ],related:['sensitivities','irrbb','treasury-risk']},
 liquidity:{title:'Liquidity and funding risk',paragraphs:[
 'Liquidity is the ability to meet payments when they fall due without incurring unacceptable losses. Funding risk concerns the availability and cost of the money used to support assets and operations. A bank can be profitable, and even have positive equity, while still lacking usable cash in the currency or location where a payment is due.',
 'This module introduces the liquidity coverage ratio and net stable funding ratio, then moves to cash survival, funding concentration, collateral and management actions. The ratios provide structured regulatory views, while a cash forecast shows when a shortage may occur. Collateral capacity and funding plans add another question: can the proposed resources actually be mobilised in time?',
 'Use the default examples to understand the distinction between a stock of assets, a dated cash flow and a potential funding action. Then change withdrawals, inflows or funding assumptions and trace the effect on the remaining buffer. The examples use selected Basel rules where stated and synthetic assumptions elsewhere. The Treasury Risk Lab extends these ideas into a broader balance-sheet decision process.'
 ],related:['treasury-risk','irrbb','yield']},
 credit:{title:'Credit risk, modelling and IFRS 9',paragraphs:[
 'Credit risk is the possibility that a borrower or counterparty will not meet its obligations as expected. The resulting loss depends on whether default occurs, how much is exposed at that time and how much is ultimately recovered. A mortgage, a credit card and a corporate loan can therefore require different assumptions even when the same high-level loss formula is used.',
 'The opening lessons introduce probability of default, loss given default, exposure at default and portfolio behaviour. You will examine dependence, concentration and migration to understand why an average expected loss does not fully describe a portfolio’s risk. The modelling lessons then explain how candidate variables are selected, how data leakage can produce misleading results, and how discrimination, calibration and stability are assessed.',
 'The accounting section introduces IFRS 9 expected credit losses, staging and significant increases in credit risk. It explains the difference between a loss associated with defaults possible in the next twelve months and losses over the remaining lifetime. Scenario weights, effective-interest discounting, revolving facilities and credit-impaired assets are treated separately because they affect the calculation in different ways.',
 'Work through the path in that order if you are new to credit risk. Use the synthetic portfolios and dataset to test explanations rather than memorise a single PD or LGD. The module distinguishes accounting estimates, internal risk measures and regulatory capital concepts, and states where specialist methods or further judgement are needed.'
 ],start:'portfolio',related:['saccr','cva','treasury-risk']},
 simm:{title:'The Standard Initial Margin Model',paragraphs:[
 'When a counterparty defaults, a derivatives portfolio cannot always be replaced or closed immediately. Its value can move during that period. Initial margin provides a separate buffer for this possible future movement, while variation margin addresses the exposure already reflected in current market values. ISDA’s Standard Initial Margin Model, usually called SIMM, estimates initial margin from a portfolio’s risk sensitivities.',
 'The module follows the calculation from signed sensitivities through permitted netting, risk weights, concentration adjustments and aggregation. It distinguishes product classes from risk classes, then examines the six risk classes individually. The optionality lessons explain why vega and curvature need additional treatment and why SIMM curvature is derived from volatility risk and expiry rather than a raw gamma input.',
 'The later lessons connect the model result to hedging decisions, additional margin, collateral transfers, two-way posting and funding. You will also examine risk-data exchange, reconciliation, backtesting and calibration changes. These practical steps explain why two firms can disagree about a number even when they appear to use the same model.',
 'Start with the distinction between initial and variation margin, and keep track of signs and units as you progress. The path references the identified ISDA methodology release. Reduced portfolios and adjustable teaching parameters are labelled, and selected prescribed values are identified separately. The applets explain the calculation but do not constitute a complete calibrated SIMM implementation.'
 ],start:'overview',related:['sensitivities','saccr','xva']},
 'treasury-risk':{title:'Treasury risk',paragraphs:[
 'Treasury connects a bank’s assets, funding and payment obligations. It makes sure that cash is available where and when it is needed, manages the reliability and cost of funding, and considers how interest rates and exchange rates affect the balance sheet. These responsibilities interact: a decision that improves earnings can increase refinancing risk, while a useful hedge can create a cash margin call.',
 'The path begins with a simple bank balance sheet and the distinction between liquidity and solvency. It then develops cash forecasting, deposit behaviour, maturity concentration and funds transfer pricing. The regulatory liquidity lessons explain HQLA, LCR and NSFR before the path turns to intraday payments, collateral, interest-rate exposure, FX funding and counterparty dependencies.',
 'The final stages explain stress testing, contingency funding, internal limits and the Internal Liquidity Adequacy Assessment Process. The ALCO exercise brings these ideas together in a balanced example bank. You will choose an asset and funding mix, observe earnings and stressed liquidity, and consider the resulting economic-value exposure.',
 'Follow the stages in order, and explain each decision in terms of cash flows, timing, currency and ownership of the risk. The objective is to develop a reasoned treasury judgement rather than optimise a single dashboard number. The examples use synthetic data and link to Basel principles and PRA guidance; their limitations remain visible beside the calculations.'
 ],start:'balance-sheet',related:['liquidity','irrbb','yield','simm']},
 regulation:{title:'FRTB Regulation Divergences',paragraphs:[
 'A regulatory topic can have different treatment across jurisdictions even when the documents use similar terminology. This module helps you examine those differences for FRTB by comparing the selected PRA rules, US notice of proposed rulemaking and Basel market-risk standard. Their legal status matters: a proposal is not an implemented rule, and an international standard is not automatically the law in every jurisdiction.',
 'Divergence Analysis places the treatment of a topic side by side, with the relevant source reference, a plain-language interpretation and the likely calculation implication. The Navigator then lets you focus on one jurisdiction and locate the relevant provision. Topics include the standardised components, internal models, modellability, profit-and-loss attribution, backtesting and governance.',
 'Choose a topic you already recognise from the FRTB module, then compare the scope, definitions and implementation dates before comparing numerical parameters. Open the linked provision when you need its full wording or exceptions. The module is a guided comparison of the identified source documents, not a live legal-status service or a substitute for reading the applicable rule in context.'
 ],related:['frtb','sensitivities']}
};
